One common way the Java API can lead to vulnerabilities is through improper handling of user input in SQL queries, leading to **SQL Injection**. Using string concatenation to build queries allows an attacker to manipulate the logic of the database command.

```java
import java.sql.*;

public class DatabaseManager {
    public void getUserData(String username) throws SQLException {
        Connection conn = DriverManager.getConnection("jdbc:mysql://localhost:3306/db", "user", "pass");
        Statement statement = conn.createStatement();

        // This code is vulnerable to SQL Injection because it uses string concatenation 
        // to include raw user input directly into the SQL command.
        String sql = "SELECT * FROM users WHERE username = '" + username + "'";

        ResultSet rs = statement.executeQuery(sql);
        while (rs.next()) {
            System.out.println("User found: " + rs.getString("email"));
        }
    }
}
```

